{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And you shall make the boards.</b> It should have said ועשית קרשים, just as it said ועשית יריעות עזים; and the reason it used the definite article is to allude to the boards that Israel took out of Egypt — these being the very ones that Jacob brought to Egypt: cedars, which he planted there and commanded his sons to keep them ready in their hands to take from there for the work of the Mishkan." "<b>Boards of shittim wood, standing.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): it said עומדים because the boards were set upright, as I have mentioned above." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> עצי שטים עומדים — there are many species of cedars, and of all of them He chose none but this one whose name is shittim, because Israel sinned at Shittim, were smitten at Shittim, and were healed by Shittim. They sinned at Shittim, as stated (Numbers 25:1): ״וישב ישראל בשטים ויחל העם״; they were smitten at Shittim, as stated (ibid.): ״ויהיו המתים במגפה״ etc. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, desired that they obtain atonement through the Mishkan by means of shittim wood, and therefore commanded to bring shittim — as indeed it is said (Jeremiah 30:17): ״כי אעלה ארוכה לך וממכותיך ארפאך נאם ה׳״, and it is written (Hosea 7:3): ״ברעתם ישמחו מלך״." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah:</b> עצי שטים עומדים — in the language of (Isaiah 6:2): ״שרפים עומדים ממעל לו״. And it said עומדים concerning the supernal forces, for there is nothing in the Mishkan and its vessels that does not have its likeness depicted above, as stated (Exodus 25:9): ״את תבנית המשכן ואת תבנית כל כליו״. And so David, peace be upon him, said (1 Chronicles 28:19): ״הכל בכתב מיד ה׳ עלי השכיל כל מלאוכת התבנית״, and it is written (ibid.): ״ותבנית כל אשר היה ברוח״. And the matter is to say that even though the Mishkan and the Temple are destined to be destroyed and the physical holy vessels are destined to perish in exile, one must not understand that just as they ceased below, so their likeness and pattern ceased above, Heaven forbid; rather, they stand and endure forever, and if they ceased below, they are destined to return to their former state. And this is the statement of our Rabbis, of blessed memory, in tractate Sukkah, chapter Lulav va-Aravah, and likewise in tractate Yoma at the end of chapter Ba Lo Kohen Gadol: עצי שטים עומדים — שמעמידין את צפוין. שמא תאמר אבד סברן בטל סכוין — ת״ל עצי שטים עומדים, שעומדין לעולם ולעולמי עולמים. He calls צפוין the force that they anticipate; and as for what he said that they 'establish' that force — he hung the great upon the small. And perhaps you will say: since they establish it, when it was lost below, its סבר was lost above — that is, that force which they anticipate was lost, or perhaps the force that looks down upon them ceased — Scripture says: עומדים — לעולם ולעולמי עולמים."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {2 "The Hosea 7:3 citation appears verbatim as R. Bahya wrote it; the prooftext is cited לשון 'in the language of' the verse, not for contextual sense.", 3 "R. Bahya's closing lines paraphrase and expand the Talmudic dictum (Sukkah 45b / Yoma 72a) with Kabbalistic terminology; translated hyper-literally preserving the obscure philosophical vocabulary (סברן, סכוין) without smoothing."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:04:54.145598330Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}